    "lawCode": "Add §31-j, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6343\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to\nproviding assistance for community gardens\n\n\n PURPOSE OF BILL :\nTo create a funding source for NYC community gardens using revenue\nfrom the sale, by the City, of former garden properties.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nAdds section 31-j to the Agriculature and Markets Law requiring that\ncities with a population of one million or more create an account, the\n\"Community Gardens Account\", to be funded with five percent of the\nproceeds from the sale or lease of property that was used as a\ncommunity garden, through an agreement with the city, within five\nyears of the sale. The fund will be used for community garden\nprograms.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nThere are over 700 community gardens currently operating in New York\nCity. These gardens provide open space, recreation, and food\nproduction for thousands of New Yorkers. Most of the gardens were\ncreated through sweat equity by neighborhood residents who volunteered\nto clear abandoned lots and create and maintain green space for use by\nseniors, children, school groups, summer camps, and, even, artists and\nmusic performers. Many of the gardens have received honors and some\nhave received national press attention.\n\nMost of the gardens are located in communities where residents do not\nhave yards or own a second home in the country where they could tend a\nplot of land. These gardens are often the only \"park\" on the street.\n\nGardens are also important for low-income citizens who are required to\nbecome more self-sufficient, yet have limited access to affordable\nfresh produce. Some of the garden sponsors donate significant\nquantities of food to feeding programs and one program allows\nparticipants to sell their NYC grown produce at the local farmers'\nmarket.\n\nIn May of 1998 the decision was made to begin selling off city-owned\ngarden parcels, many of which had been gardens for 10 or more years.\nMost residents and community organizations oppose these sales. Since\nthe City will benefit from the sale of land that had been transformed\nby gardeners it is only appropriate that some of the proceeds be\nreinvested in community gardens. This bill would provide support for\nthe continuation of the proud tradition of hard-working urban\nresidents taking the initiative to beautify and improve their city.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n1999/00 A.6745; 2001/02 A.1173; 2003/04 A.463; 2005/06 A.2543; 2007/08\nA.2447\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS :\nWould affect the use, by cities with a population of one million or\nmore, of money received from community garden property sales.\n\n REGULATION IMPACT :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nImmediately.\n",
