    "lawCode": "Add §31-j, Ag & Mkts L",
    "lawSection": "Agriculture and Markets Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S6343A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the agriculture and markets law, in relation to\nproviding assistance for community gardens\n\n\n PURPOSE OF BILL :\nThe purpose of this bill is to help support municipal community\ngardens. This bill allocates a small portion of revenue generated by\nthe sale, transfer or lease of such former community gardens and\nretains such funds for the continued support of local community\ngardens. The municipality may use the 10% of retained funds to\npurchase additional community garden lands, install capital\nimprovements to other municipally owned community gardens, or to\nprovide supplies and materials to support community garden activities.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1: Adds Agriculture & Markets Law section 31-j which requires\nthat municipalities create an account, the \"Community Gardens\nAccount\", to be funded with ten percent of the proceeds from the sale,\ntransfer or lease of property that was used as a community garden. The\nfund would only need to be established if such community garden was\nsold, transferred or leased. The fund will be used for community\ngarden programs.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nCommunity gardens provide open space, recreation, and food production\nopportunities for thousands of New Yorkers and help to beautify\ncommunities. Most of these gardens were created through sweat equity\nby neighborhood residents who volunteered to clear abandoned lots and\ncreate and maintain green space for use by seniors, children, school\ngroups, and summer camps. Many of the gardens have received honors and\nsome have received national press attention.\n\nMost of the gardens are located in communities where residents do not\nhave yards or own a second home in the country where they could tend a\nplot of land. These gardens are often the only \"park\" on the street.\nEncouraging the maintenance of these gardens should help to bring\nnature to urban neighborhoods where the existence of nature is not\nself evident.\n\nGardens are also important for low-income citizens who, by working in\na community garden, can become more self-sufficient by growing their\nown fresh produce. Further, money that would have been used to\npurchase food can be used for other needs. Some of the garden sponsors\ndonate significant quantities of food to feeding programs to help\nothers.\n\nSince such municipality benefits from the sale of land (that was\npreviously vacant and abandoned property) that had been transformed by\ngardeners, it is appropriate that some of the proceeds be reinvested\nback into community gardens. This bill provides support for the\ncontinuation of the proud tradition of hard-working urban residents\ntaking the initiative to beautify and improve their city.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\nNew bill.\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect on the 180th day after it shall have become\nlaw.\n",
